<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Advance Australia Fair</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1878">1878</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Advance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> Fair is the national anthem of Australia, written by Peter Dodds McCormick and first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> in 1878. After being chosen in a national poll in 1977, it was proclaimed the national anthem in 1984, replacing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>, which remained the royal anthem. Its words were changed in 2021 from 'young and free' to 'one and free'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/advance-australia-fair.jpg" alt="Advance Australia Fair" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Dodds McCormick · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Advance_Australia_Fair_(1878).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Peter Dodds McCormick (1833–1916), a Scottish-born Australian composer, published the song in early December 1878 under the pen-name Amicus. It was first sung by Andrew Fairfax, with a concert band conducted by McCormick, at a function of the Highland Society of New South Wales in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> on 30 November 1878, Saint Andrew's Day. In a letter of 1 August 1913, McCormick said he had attended a concert at Sydney's Exhibition Building where various national anthems were played and felt aggravated that there was not one note for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. He wrote the first verse on the bus home and set it to music, and said the words and music came with ease.</p><p>The song grew in popularity, and an amended version was sung by a choir of around 10,000 at the inauguration of the Commonwealth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> on 1 January 1901. In 1907 the Australian Government awarded McCormick £100. The Australian Broadcasting Commission used it to announce its radio news bulletins from 1942 to 1952.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The original song had four verses. The second edition of 1879 begins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>'s sons let us rejoice, For we are young and free, and its second verse told of Cook raising Old England's flag, while its later verses spoke of Britannia and of rousing to arms against a foreign foe.</p><p>For the 1984 adoption the text was cut to two verses. The first was kept largely as the original, with the first line changed to Australians all let us rejoice, and the second is a modified form of the third verse sung at Federation in 1901, which begins Beneath our radiant Southern Cross. On 1 January 2021 the word young in the second line was replaced by one, in recognition of the legacy of Indigenous Australians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Whitlam government replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a> with Advance Australia Fair as the official national anthem in 1974, after an indicative opinion survey. The Fraser government restored God Save the Queen in January 1976 alongside three national songs: Advance Australia Fair, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waltzing-matilda">Waltzing Matilda</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Australia. A plebiscite in 1977 to choose the national song preferred Advance Australia Fair, and the Hawke government proclaimed it the national anthem in 1984. God Save the Queen became the royal anthem.</p><p>The song has drawn criticism for its archaic word girt and for a melody some call dull. It has also been criticised for failing to acknowledge Indigenous peoples; the line for we are young and free was said to ignore their long history. Versions in the Dharug language have been written, by Jacqueline Troy in 2003 and by Richard Green, performed in 2010. The version most often performed at official functions is a 1986 arrangement by Thomas Tycho.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Advance Australia Fair” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A separate Australian citizenship was created in 1949, and <b>Advance Australia Fair</b> replaced God Save the Queen as the national anthem in 1984.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The Whitlam government held a competition in 1973 that produced no music and a shortlist of lyrics that critics derided, and then an opinion poll of about 60,000 people that offered only &quot;<b>Advance Australia Fair</b>&quot;, &quot;Waltzing Matilda&quot; and &quot;Song of Australia&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/advance-australia-fair">Advance Australia Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>In the national plebiscite of 1977 it was one of four choices for Australia's national song, with &quot;Waltzing Matilda&quot;, the then anthem &quot;God Save the Queen&quot; and &quot;<b>Advance Australia Fair</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-song-of-australia">The Song of Australia</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1977 plebiscite on a national song it came second with about 28%, behind &quot;<b>Advance Australia Fair</b>&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/waltzing-matilda">Waltzing Matilda</a></li></ul></section>
